From 7dad16f79061fbad8ff7bc50a60b16909d5fe126 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 15:53:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count item 1-1's capture-free deferrable population, and
 refuse the shortcut

Item 1-1's remaining half defers a function body's expression-tree construction
to first invocation. It is worth 33.6-63.9% of compile over a population that is
84-99.7% never invoked, and it has one named obstacle: a captured name's index in
the enclosing lambda's Box[] is decided by LambdaRewriter FROM THE TREE, and a
deferred body has none. 0101 built and priced the walk that would make the layout
addressable.

What nobody had counted is how many sites need the layout at all. A function
whose free names resolve to nothing an enclosing scope holds captures nothing, is
handed no boxes, and could be deferred with the mechanism that already exists.

Counted, over 5 762 function sites on the six corpora: 728 are capture-free,
12.6% -- 7.4% on Mandreel, 39.7% on the flattest corpus. So the capture mechanism
is not an optional extra for a minority; 87.3% of sites need it, and it is what
the item is. Direct eval, the risk the item's text spends most words on, refuses
7 sites of 5 762.

And the reading that looked like an opening is refused by the counter built to
test it. Mandreel is 1 364 top-level declarations, and its 7 605 bound free names
are only 165 function-owned -- a script's top-level var is a property of the
global object per spec, so those looked deferrable for nothing. They are not:
cellBacked equals bound EXACTLY on all six corpora, 15 118 of 15 118, because
this engine gives a program-level binding a CLR local in the program lambda like
any other. A spec-level fact about where a binding lives is not a fact about
where the compiler puts it.

The instrument was made to discriminate before it was pointed anywhere, which is
what makes that equality mean something rather than being a claim about the
counter:

  * 18 fixtures, the negatives being the load-bearing half. The same body text in
    two enclosing scopes gives two different verdicts, which no identifier scan
    can do; a parameter or an inner var sharing the outer spelling captures
    nothing, which is the distinction NestedFunctionScanner cannot make.
  * cellBacked is shown to separate from bound before the equality is reported --
    a named function expression's own name binds with no CLR local, and reads
    1 bound / 0 cellBacked against an ordinary local's 1 / 1.
  * Deliberately breaking the resolver fails exactly the four capture-detecting
    fixtures and leaves the other eleven green.

The denominator is cross-checked rather than asserted: classified sites match
--compile-profile's own function count exactly on four corpora, and differ by +2
and +1 on the two that evaluate a CodeLoad epilogue, which contains exactly 2 and
1 inline functions.

The probe reads the enclosing scope through a new side-effect-free
TryResolveBinding rather than GetVariable, because GetVariable sets
RootScope.HasOuterFunctionCaptures and that flag is a conjunct of the tiering
gate -- a probe built on it would turn tiering off for functions it merely asked
about. Pinned by a fixture that runs the same program on both settings.

Off by default (BROILER_JS_DEFER_TREE_COUNT=1), a compile-time counter on the
same terms as 0094's and 0097's, and it changes no emission on any setting.

